WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.037
This slide shows the learning path for

00:00:02.063 --> 00:00:05.658
Module 6 We will begin with imports

00:00:06.579 --> 00:00:08.724
An import allows Python to use code

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.342
that is stored somewhere else We will

00:00:11.368 --> 00:00:13.513
start with modules that are already included

00:00:13.539 --> 00:00:16.355
with Python such as math random datetime

00:00:16.539 --> 00:00:19.658
and statistics Next we will look at

00:00:19.684 --> 00:00:22.460
how to create our own module This

00:00:22.487 --> 00:00:24.263
is an important step because it shows

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:26.750
how one Python file can provide reusable

00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:30.355
functions to another Python file After that

00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:33.645
we will discuss packages A package is

00:00:33.671 --> 00:00:36.329
a folder that groups related modules together

00:00:37.263 --> 00:00:39.513
We will also distinguish between a module

00:00:39.711 --> 00:00:42.684
a package and a library This will

00:00:42.697 --> 00:00:44.513
prepare you for later modules when we

00:00:44.526 --> 00:00:47.053
begin using larger tools such as NumPy

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:50.592
and Pandas Then we move into object-oriented

00:00:50.618 --> 00:00:55.092
programming We will introduce classes objects
attributes

00:00:55.211 --> 00:00:58.158
methods and the word self We will

00:00:58.184 --> 00:01:01.579
keep these ideas simple and practical You

00:01:01.592 --> 00:01:03.303
will also see how one class can

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:07.316
create multiple objects For example one Product

00:01:07.342 --> 00:01:09.539
class can create a laptop object a

00:01:09.553 --> 00:01:12.395
monitor object and many other product objects

00:01:13.316 --> 00:01:15.434
Each object can store its own values

00:01:15.461 --> 00:01:18.882
while using the same class definition Finally

00:01:19.092 --> 00:01:22.092
we will compare functions dictionaries modules and

00:01:22.118 --> 00:01:24.750
classes The goal is to help you

00:01:24.776 --> 00:01:26.987
decide which structure is appropriate for a

00:01:27.013 --> 00:01:30.289
particular task The key idea for the

00:01:30.316 --> 00:01:33.895
entire module is organization We are learning

00:01:33.921 --> 00:01:35.421
how to give related code a clear

00:01:35.447 --> 00:01:37.408
home so that an analytics project can

00:01:37.421 --> 00:01:39.658
grow without becoming one large and confusing

00:01:39.671 --> 00:01:40.132
script
